TAM-LAST - THE TAMLINUX TERMINAL

tam-last is our own Wayland terminal emulator, written in C. It is built
fresh, from the ground up, and it learns from the best ideas and the best
code of foot, kitty, st, havoc, ghostty, alacritty, wezterm and every
other terminal we can find. Where another terminal's code works well, we
take it and refactor it into tam-last's own shape. Where it doesn't, we
leave it out. tam-last is not a fork or a clone of any of them.

Follows the tam-idol 0.1.0 standard.


WHY

  We want full control over how key presses get from evdev to our TUI
  apps. Between a finger on a key and a program reading a byte there are
  a keyboard's firmware, the kernel's input layer, the compositor, a
  keymap, the terminal's key encoding, the kernel's tty layer, and the
  program's own decoder. Today most of those belong to someone else. With
  tam-pane (our compositor), tam-last (our terminal), libtam's key decoder
  and tam-clam (our shell), every hop on the path is code we can read,
  change and test.

  And we are having fun. The flow of information between human interfaces
  and our apps is a good story, and tam-last is a way to dive into it:
  every hop is documented in arch/key-path.md, and tam-last will show its
  own part of the path as you type (P2).


WHAT IT DOES

  0.0.2 opens a window, runs your shell, draws text with a TrueType font,
  and takes keyboard input:

  - Its own Wayland client code: the socket, message framing and
    file-descriptor passing are written here. There is no libwayland.
  - Its own TrueType rasterizer: it reads the font file's glyf outlines
    and draws them antialiased, with the x-height snapped to the pixel
    grid. There is no FreeType and no fontconfig. JetBrains Mono at 9
    points makes 7 x 17 pixel cells, as in foot. On scaled outputs (1.25,
    say) it draws at the output's real resolution, through the
    fractional-scale and viewporter protocols, so text stays sharp.
  - Its own keymap: the compositor's evdev key codes are turned into
    characters by tables compiled into tam-last (a US layout for now).
    There is no xkbcommon.
  - The legacy xterm key encoding, which every terminal program reads.
  - The escape sequences a shell and `ls --color` need: cursor movement,
    erasing, inserting and deleting characters and lines, 16, 256 and
    direct colors, bold, underline, inverse, and answers to cursor and
    device queries. Wide characters take two cells.

  Full-screen programs (vim, htop, yazi), scrollback, the clipboard and
  the kitty keyboard protocol come next; see ROADMAP.md.


HOW IT IS BUILT

  - Every dependency has to earn its place. The goal is the C library
    (libc and libm) and libtam, and nothing else, and 0.0.2 is there:
    `readelf -d build/tam-last` lists only libc.so.6 and libm.so.6.
  - Small modules with one job each, and explicit state; the only global
    is the pipe the SIGCHLD handler writes to.
  - Everything from outside is untrusted: compositor messages, font files
    and program output are bounds-checked, with limits on sizes and
    counts, and fuzzed in the tests.
  - Energy is a feature. An idle terminal sleeps in poll() with no
    timeout; it draws only when something changed, at most once per
    frame the compositor asks for. No cursor blink.
  - Pure logic (the wire format, the parser and screen, the font code, the
    key encoding) runs without a display and is unit tested.
  - Plain Make. There is no other build system.

  arch/principles.md has the full set of principles.


BUILDING

  Needs: a C11 compiler, make, and libtam installed under LIBTAM_PREFIX
  (default $HOME/.local). Nothing else.

    make              build build/tam-last
    make test         unit tests, then the headless check
    make sanitize     the tests again, built with ASan and UBSan
    make install      install to PREFIX (default $HOME/.local)
    make uninstall    remove the installed binary
    make clean        remove build/

  The headless check runs tam-pane (from ../tam-pane/build, or TAM_PANE)
  on wlroots' headless backend, so it never touches the running desktop.
  It is skipped when there is no tam-pane build.


USING IT

    tam-last                      run $SHELL
    tam-last -e htop              run a command instead
    tam-last -f FONT.ttf -s 11    another TrueType font, another size

  The font is the --font file, else $TAM_LAST_FONT, else the first
  installed of JetBrains Mono (Nerd Font), DejaVu Sans Mono, Liberation
  Mono and Noto Sans Mono. Fonts with CFF outlines (.otf) are not read
  yet.


WHERE THINGS ARE

  src/, include/   the terminal (one module per file)
    wl/            Wayland wire protocol and requests
    font/          TrueType parsing, rasterizing, glyph cache
    keys/          keyboard state, layout and key encoding
  tests/           unit tests and the headless check
  protocols/       the Wayland protocol files our requests follow
  ideas/           ideas we collect from other terminals
  arch/            architecture: principles, the key path, data
                   structures, dependencies, evaluations
  plans/           one plan per work phase (P1, P2, ...)
  bugs/            one record per bug (B1, B2, ...)
  reviews/         one record per review (R1, R2, ...)
  work/            WHATNOW (read it first: what is next) and one record
                   per work session (W1, W2, ...)
  tools/           helper scripts for developing tam-last
  ROADMAP.md       milestones with their version numbers
  UPSTREAM.md      every piece of code we took from another project
  AI-BLAME.md      how AI work is recorded here
  COPYRIGHT        copyright and license notices
  LICENSE          GNU General Public License, version 3


